    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d502">

                <pb facs="0641" n="529" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg529"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/397</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Department of State to the Chinese
                        Legation</hi><anchor n="33" xml:id="d502-f1"/></head>
                <p>The Silver Purchase Act<anchor n="34" xml:id="d502-f2"/> is mandatory. The
                    American Government cannot make an agreement whether informal or formal in
                    impairment of its provisions. In administering it, this Government endeavors to
                    avoid unnecessarily occasioning hardship to any country or interest. Our present
                    purchasing procedure is believed to take into account the ideas comprised in the
                    suggestions offered in the Chinese Government’s communication under reference.
                    We will expect, immediately upon receipt of the “Outline of Further Measures”,
                    to give the contents of the outline careful consideration.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1935-01-26">January 26,
                            1935</date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d502-f1" n="33" type="source" xml:id="d502-f1-content">
                <p>Informal message handed to the Chinese Minister, January 26, by the Chief of the
                    Division of Far Eastern Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d502-f2" n="34" xml:id="d502-f2-content">
                <p>Approved June 19, 1934; 48 Stat. 1178.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
